The '''Act of Standing Military of 1837''' was a Sartan parliamentary reform that created a formal national military. Before the act, the kingdom relied heavily on serf conscripts and on the lords themselves to lend their own forces for internal defence and local order. The new law replaced that arrangement with a more centralised military structure under the authority of the state.

== Background ==

The act followed the [[Sartan Peasants Revolution of 1837]], when a major peasant uprising in and around [[Leona]] exposed the weakness of Sarta's existing security system. The old arrangement depended on estate levies, local lordships, and temporary mobilisation rather than a permanent national force. That system was increasingly difficult to sustain in a kingdom facing urban growth, rural unrest, and wider administrative reform.

The lords' parliament saw the need for a more reliable military structure, particularly because Sarta still relied on [[Volisania]] for broader strategic protection and could not depend entirely on improvised local forces.

== Provisions ==

The Act of Standing Military of 1837 established a formal national military with clearer recruitment, command, and administrative oversight. It reduced the kingdom's dependence on individual lordships for raising forces and shifted defence toward a central state institution.

The reform did not immediately produce a large modern army, but it created the legal basis for a standing military and regularised the chain of command. It was part of the same broader state-building process that also produced the [[Constitution of 1812]] and the [[Act on Free Movement of 1838]].

== Significance ==

The act marked an important turning point in Sartan governance. It signalled that the kingdom was moving away from an estate-based military order and toward a more centralised national structure. In later Sartan political memory, the reform was closely associated with the urban unrest and peasant pressure of the late 1830s.

Its passage also reinforced the authority of the lords' parliament, which remained the real centre of domestic power even as the king retained his role as a figurehead of stability.

